---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import EditorialLocaleMasthead from "../../../components/EditorialLocaleMasthead.astro";
import EditorialArticle from "../../../components/EditorialArticle.astro";
import { buildBreadcrumbJsonLd, buildPersonJsonLd, filterPostsForLang, getAllPosts, getPostSlug } from "../../../utils/blog";
import "../../../styles/editorial-resources.css";
const lang = "en";
const posts = filterPostsForLang(await getAllPosts(), lang).filter(post => !post.data.noindex);
const starts = ["echecs-et-memoire", "echecs-et-concentration", "echecs-et-la-triche"].flatMap(slug => {
  const post = posts.find(p => getPostSlug(p) === slug);
  return post ? [post] : [];
});
const sections = [{"id": "background", "label": "My background"}, {"id": "perspective", "label": "Editorial approach"}, {"id": "reading", "label": "Reading and resources"}, {"id": "method", "label": "Research and writing"}, {"id": "corrections", "label": "Discussion and corrections"}, {"id": "independence", "label": "Independence and funding"}];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About: the project and the approach | A Left-Hander’s Blog"
  description="Meet Le_Gaucher and discover the editorial approach behind a personal chess publication exploring science, psychology and society from Guadeloupe."
  canonicalPath="/en/about/"
  lang={lang}
  frPath="/fr/about/"
  enPath="/en/about/"
  ptBrPath="/pt-br/about/"
  structuredData={[
    buildBreadcrumbJsonLd([{name:"Home",path:"/en/"},{name:"About",path:"/en/about/"}]),
    buildPersonJsonLd(lang),
  ]}
>
  <article class="resource-page"><div class="container">
    <header class="resource-header">
      <EditorialLocaleMasthead lang={lang} active="about" />
      <div class="resource-heading"><span class="resource-kicker">About · The project and its author</span><h1>{"Understanding chess.\nBroadening the perspective."}</h1><p>Chess is a game, a subject of research and a cultural practice. A Left-Hander’s Blog explores the connections between them: how we learn, make decisions and play together.</p></div>
    </header>
    <div class="about-layout">
      <div class="about-prose">
        <section id="background" aria-labelledby="background-title">
          <span class="resource-kicker">01 · My background</span>
          <h2 id="background-title">A player, a set of questions, a publication.</h2>
          <p>I write as <strong>Le_Gaucher</strong>. I am a chess player and computer science student based in Le Gosier, Guadeloupe. This blog is a personal project: I am responsible for its writing, editorial choices and development.</p>
<p>At 16, I was asked to lead a chess club. I had a great deal to learn. I began researching, reading and trying to understand what helps a player improve. How do you explain an idea? How do you move from recognising a position to understanding it?</p>
<p>Those questions soon reached beyond openings and combinations. Why do some positions stay in our memory? What happens to our judgement under pressure? How can a game tell us something about a period, a culture or a way of thinking?</p>
<p>I first wrote on Chess.com before building a space of my own. A Left-Hander’s Blog grew out of that desire to pursue questions in depth, with the freedom to connect disciplines and take the time to explain.</p>
<p class="about-personal"><strong>Why “Left-Hander”?</strong> Because I am one. “Gaucher” is French for left-handed. The name is personal; the perspective develops through reading, playing and discussion.</p>
        </section>
        <section id="perspective" aria-labelledby="perspective-title">
          <span class="resource-kicker">02 · Editorial approach</span>
          <h2 id="perspective-title">Three perspectives on the same board.</h2>
          <p>A chess game involves calculation, memory and emotion. It also takes place in a social setting: a club, a school, an online platform or a competition. I explore that range through three complementary sections.</p>
<div class="about-lenses">
<div><h3><a href="/en/science/">Science <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></h3><p>Understanding how the game works through memory, learning, mathematics, algorithms and artificial intelligence. The aim is to explain how a result was obtained and what it actually allows us to conclude about chess.</p></div>
<div><h3><a href="/en/esprit/">Mind <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></h3><p>Examining the psychology of the player: attention, confidence, cognitive biases, motivation and the response to defeat. These ideas can help you reflect on your play and improvement, while keeping the limits of their practical applications clear.</p></div>
<div><h3><a href="/en/societe/">Society <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></h3><p>Placing chess in the wider world: history, education, inequality, money, culture and trust in competition. The game offers a way to examine the institutions and perceptions that surround it.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The underlying approach stays the same: begin with a precise question, define the concepts that matter, develop an argument and test it against examples. My ambition is to create in-depth articles you can return to when exploring a subject or pursuing your own research.</p>
        </section>
        <section id="reading" aria-labelledby="reading-title">
          <span class="resource-kicker">03 · Reading and resources</span>
          <h2 id="reading-title">Different ways to go deeper.</h2>
          <p>You might read this site out of curiosity, to reflect on your chess practice or to prepare a school project. The formats serve those different purposes without assuming that you already know every concept.</p>
<dl class="about-formats">
<div><dt><a href="/en/series/">The series</a></dt><dd>Connected articles that build an understanding of a subject step by step. The English edition includes the psychology of chess series, exploring memory, motivation, judgement and social influence.</dd></div>
<div><dt><a href="/en/blog/">The article archive</a></dt><dd>Browse the published articles or use <a href="/en/recherche/">search</a> to find a concept, a question or a topic that crosses the three sections.</dd></div>
<div><dt><a href="/fr/dissertations/" hreflang="fr">Essays in French</a></dt><dd>Extended arguments about winning, the value of a player and equality in the game, developed through objections and conceptual distinctions. These essays are currently available in French.</dd></div>
<div><dt><a href="/fr/grand-oral/" hreflang="fr">Grand oral guides in French</a></dt><dd>Topics and resources connecting chess to subjects studied for the French baccalauréat oral examination. When preparing for my own exam, I was looking for those connections. These guides help students develop their own arguments and prepare for the examiners’ questions; they are available in French.</dd></div>
<div><dt><a href="/fr/glossaire/" hreflang="fr">The glossary</a> and <a href="/fr/infographie/" hreflang="fr">the Atlas</a> <span class="about-language-note">(in French)</span></dt><dd>Reference points for looking up a concept, exploring the site’s themes and choosing what to read next. The English archive remains the starting point for articles in English.</dd></div>
</dl>
        </section>
        <section id="method" aria-labelledby="method-title">
          <span class="resource-kicker">04 · Research and writing</span>
          <h2 id="method-title">From sources to an argument.</h2>
          <p>I write from my position as a player and computer science student. An article’s credibility should be open to scrutiny through its sources, explanations and examples. These are the principles that guide the work.</p>
<ol class="about-method">
<li><strong>Frame the question.</strong><span>A playing experience, a frequently repeated claim or a research finding can open a subject. I first identify the question to answer and the concepts needed to understand it.</span></li>
<li><strong>Return to the publications.</strong><span>I look for the studies and documents behind a claim. Research tools, including Consensus AI, help me find leads; they do not replace examining the sources. An abstract or a catalogue record does not provide the same access to the findings as the full text.</span></li>
<li><strong>Explain what the findings support.</strong><span>A correlation, an experiment and a personal account answer different questions. I aim to explain the population studied, the context and the limitations that matter to the argument. Applying a general finding to chess advice also requires justification.</span></li>
<li><strong>Test ideas against the game.</strong><span>Examples, positions and variations help explain a mechanism or examine an idea. An illustrative example created for an article should be identifiable as such. A personal recommendation should be distinguishable from an established finding.</span></li>
<li><strong>Make the reasoning traceable.</strong><span>References should let you find the work being discussed. The aim is to help you follow the explanation, examine its limits and consult the sources to form your own judgement.</span></li>
</ol>
        </section>
        <section id="corrections" aria-labelledby="corrections-title">
          <span class="resource-kicker">05 · Discussion and corrections</span>
          <h2 id="corrections-title">There is always room to improve a text.</h2>
          <p>This site develops over time. A stronger source, a counterexample or a calculation error can call for an explanation to be revised. I aim to correct mistakes and expand articles while preserving what helps readers understand.</p>
<p>To report a problem, send me the article link, the relevant passage and, if you have one, a source or chess variation that allows the point to be checked. A reasoned objection is a useful contribution too: it helps clarify what an article needs to demonstrate.</p>
<p>You can also suggest a question to explore, share a resource or get in touch about an educational project through the <a href="/en/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
        </section>
        <section id="independence" aria-labelledby="independence-title">
          <span class="resource-kicker">06 · Independence and funding</span>
          <h2 id="independence-title">A personal project, freely accessible.</h2>
          <p>I fund this site myself. Articles are free to read, with no compulsory registration. I choose the subjects and remain responsible for the published texts.</p>
<p>The site may display advertising. Some articles contain affiliate links, identified where they appear: a purchase through one of those links may earn me a commission at no extra cost to you. No article is commissioned, reviewed or paid for by an advertiser.</p>
<p class="about-legal">The <a href="/en/legal-notice/">legal notice</a> explains these arrangements. The <a href="/en/privacy-policy/">privacy policy</a> describes the data and services used by the site.</p>
        </section>
        <div class="about-signature"><strong>Le_Gaucher</strong><span>Le Gosier, Guadeloupe</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="author-note" aria-labelledby="author-title">
        <img src="/images/logo.svg" alt="" width="66" height="92" />
        <span class="resource-kicker">The author</span>
        <h2 id="author-title">Le_Gaucher<span>.</span></h2>
        <p>Chess player.<br />Computer science student.<br />Author of A Left-Hander’s Blog.</p>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Based in</dt><dd>Le Gosier, Guadeloupe</dd></div>
          <div><dt>The project</dt><dd>A personal publication devoted to chess.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>The connecting thread</dt><dd>Science, mind and society.</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <a class="resource-link" href="https://www.chess.com/member/le_gaucher" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">My Chess.com profile ↗<span class="resource-sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
        <nav aria-label="On this page">
          <span class="resource-kicker">On this page</span>
          {sections.map((section, index) => <a href={`#${section.id}`}><span aria-hidden="true">{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>{section.label}</a>)}
        </nav>
        <a class="author-contact" href="/en/contact/">Get in touch <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </aside>
    </div>
    {starts.length > 0 && <section class="resource-section" aria-labelledby="starts-title">
      <div class="resource-section-head"><h2 id="starts-title">Get to know the blog</h2><span>Three articles, three perspectives</span></div>
      <p class="about-starts-intro">Memory to explore learning, concentration to reflect on your play, and cheating to examine trust: three starting points for discovering the project.</p>
      <div class="about-starts">{starts.map(post => <EditorialArticle post={post} lang={lang} variant="feature" showImageCredit={true} />)}</div>
    </section>}
    <div class="resource-end"><div><span class="resource-kicker">Keep the conversation going</span><p>A question, a correction or an idea to explore?</p></div><a class="resource-button" href="mailto:contact@blogdungaucher.com">Write to me <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
  </div></article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-layout { display:grid; grid-template-columns:minmax(0,2.1fr) minmax(0,1fr); gap:5rem; align-items:start; border-top:2px solid var(--text-main); padding-top:2rem; margin-bottom:3.5rem; }
  .about-prose { max-width:47rem; min-width:0; }
  .about-prose section { padding-bottom:2.5rem; scroll-margin-top:6rem; }
  .about-prose section+section { border-top:1px solid var(--border-strong); padding-top:2rem; }
  .about-prose h2 { font:600 1.85rem/1.2 var(--font-serif); letter-spacing:-.025em; margin:.7rem 0 1.2rem; text-wrap:pretty; }
  .about-prose p { font-size:1rem; line-height:1.85; color:var(--text-muted); margin:0 0 1rem; }
  .about-prose strong { color:var(--text-main); font-weight:600; }
  .about-prose a { color:var(--green); text-decoration:underline; text-underline-offset:.2em; }
  .about-prose a:hover { text-decoration-thickness:2px; }
  .about-prose .about-personal { background:var(--bg-warm); border-left:3px solid var(--green); padding:1rem 1.25rem; margin:1.5rem 0 0; font-size:.92rem; }
  .about-lenses { margin:1.5rem 0; border-top:1px solid var(--border-strong); }
  .about-lenses>div { padding:1.1rem 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border-strong); }
  .about-lenses h3 { margin:0 0 .5rem; font:600 1.35rem/1.3 var(--font-serif); }
  .about-lenses h3 a { display:flex; justify-content:space-between; align-items:center; text-decoration:none; }
  .about-lenses h3 span { font-family:var(--font-body); font-size:.9rem; }
  .about-lenses p { margin:0; font-size:.94rem; line-height:1.75; }
  .about-formats { margin:1.5rem 0 0; }
  .about-formats>div { padding:1.1rem 0; border-top:1px solid var(--border-strong); }
  .about-language-note { font-weight:400; color:var(--text-dim); }
  .about-formats dt { font-size:.96rem; font-weight:600; }
  .about-formats dd { margin:.55rem 0 0; color:var(--text-muted); font-size:.94rem; line-height:1.75; }
  .about-method { padding:0; list-style:none; counter-reset:method; margin:1.5rem 0 0; }
  .about-method li { display:grid; grid-template-columns:1.4rem 1fr; gap:.4rem 1rem; padding:1.15rem 0; border-top:1px solid var(--border-strong); counter-increment:method; }
  .about-method li::before { content:counter(method,decimal-leading-zero); font-size:.75rem; color:var(--green); grid-row:span 2; padding-top:.15rem; }
  .about-method strong { font-size:.96rem; }
  .about-method span { font-size:.94rem; line-height:1.75; color:var(--text-muted); }
  .about-prose .about-legal { font-size:.87rem; }
  .about-signature { display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:.35rem; }
  .about-signature strong { font:500 1.55rem var(--font-serif); }
  .about-signature span { font-size:.78rem; color:var(--text-dim); }
  .author-note { background:var(--bg-warm); padding:2rem; border-top:3px solid var(--green); }
  .author-note>img { display:block; margin-bottom:1.5rem; width:56px; height:78px; }
  :global([data-theme="dark"]) .author-note>img { filter:invert(1); }
  .author-note h2 { font:600 1.8rem var(--font-serif); letter-spacing:-.035em; margin:.6rem 0 1rem; }
  .author-note h2 span { color:var(--green); }
  .author-note p { font-size:.93rem; line-height:1.8; color:var(--text-muted); margin:0 0 1.5rem; }
  .author-note dl { margin:0 0 1.25rem; }
  .author-note dl>div { padding:.75rem 0; border-top:1px solid var(--border-strong); }
  .author-note dt { font-size:.66rem; letter-spacing:.08em; text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--text-dim); }
  .author-note dd { margin:.3rem 0 0; font-size:.86rem; line-height:1.6; }
  .author-note nav { display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:.9rem; border-top:1px solid var(--border-strong); margin-top:1.5rem; padding-top:1.25rem; }
  .author-note nav a { display:flex; gap:.75rem; font-size:.8rem; color:var(--text-muted); }
  .author-note nav a>span { color:var(--green); font-size:.68rem; padding-top:.12rem; }
  .author-note nav a:hover { color:var(--green); }
  .author-contact { display:flex; justify-content:space-between; border-top:1px solid var(--border-strong); margin-top:1.5rem; padding-top:1rem; color:var(--green); font-size:.85rem; font-weight:600; }
  .about-starts-intro { max-width:65ch; color:var(--text-muted); font-size:.95rem; line-height:1.75; margin:0 0 1.75rem; }
  .about-starts { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr)); gap:2rem; }
  .about-starts>:global(article) { min-width:0; }
  @media(min-width:900px) and (min-height:1000px) { .author-note { position:sticky; top:5rem; } }
  @media(max-width:899px) { .about-layout { gap:2rem; grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.7fr) minmax(0,1fr); } .author-note { padding:1.25rem; } }
  @media(max-width:639px) {
    .about-layout { grid-template-columns:1fr; gap:2rem; }
    .author-note { grid-row:1; }
    .author-note>img { float:right; width:40px; height:56px; margin:0 0 1rem 1rem; }
    .author-note p { margin-bottom:1rem; }
    .author-note dl { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr)); gap:0 1rem; }
    .author-note dl>div:last-child { grid-column:1 / -1; }
    .author-note nav { gap:.8rem; }
    .about-starts { grid-template-columns:1fr; gap:1.75rem; }
    .about-prose h2 { font-size:1.65rem; }
  }
</style>
